hints:
Can reproduce with MRP in 4.6.stable

Profiling (1k samples/s) points out a hotspot: 
<img width="633" height="322" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/6ebf0c5d-15f3-40c6-90ff-6cad5ca90cc1" />

Two notable reasons why it can be slow:
1. The method `EditorNode::update_resource_count` is called by `SceneTreeEditor::_update_node_subtree` which can be called for every node in a scene switched to, so up to ~2000 times when switching to scene_1.
2. The method unconditionally emits the signal "resource_counter_changed", which in the end translate to **multiple** calls to `FontFile::_get_property_list`, which is already relatively expensive. It involves dumping every cached glyph of every cached font size of every cache, returning a list of >200 properties in this case, taking around 0.5 ms on my machine.

But I did not figure out under exactly what conditions, do reason 1 no longer hold. These code are indeed updated between the good and bad version, by 804188d320a, so maybe @SatLess can shed some light on this issue?

P.S. an easy workaround is to only emit the signal when `resource_count` actually changes, on the assumption that it's the underlying data that matters, no the mere fact that the update function has been called. I tested locally and it eliminates the freeze.
```diff
diff --git a/editor/editor_node.cpp b/editor/editor_node.cpp
index 4b048b7d59..4bbfd52600 100644
--- a/editor/editor_node.cpp
+++ b/editor/editor_node.cpp
@@ -1918,18 +1918,23 @@ void EditorNode::update_resource_count(Node *p_node, bool p_remove) {
        List<Ref<Resource>> res_list;
        gather_resources(p_node, res_list, true);

+       bool changed = false;
        for (Ref<Resource> &R : res_list) {
                List<Node *>::Element *E = resource_count[R].find(p_node);
                if (E) {
                        if (p_remove) {
                                resource_count[R].erase(E);
+                               changed = true;
                        }
                } else {
                        resource_count[R].push_back(p_node);
+                       changed = true;
                }
        }

-       emit_signal(SNAME("resource_counter_changed"));
+       if (changed) {
+               emit_signal(SNAME("resource_counter_changed"));
+       }
 }
```

As far as I was able to test it, it seems it slows down more due to the sheer amount of Nodes in the scene. Even if you remove `EditorNode::update_resource_count` call from `SceneTreeEditor::_update_node_subtree`, it's still considerably slower than 4.5 (though the freeze was still there)

However, emitting the signal indiscriminately causes Godot to almost crash on my machine, so It's definitely related to a loss in performance.  Adding a check here seems appropriate.

Can you check again in beta2? It should be better.

I can confirm this on 4.7.beta 860821708 when editing Extra Spacing while `scene_1` is the current scene tab:

> Godot v4.7.beta (860821708) - Fedora Linux 44 (KDE Plasma Desktop Edition) on X11 - X11 display driver, Multi-window, 1 monitor - Vulkan (Forward+) - dedicated NVIDIA GeForce RTX 5090 (nvidia; 595.71.05) - AMD Ryzen 9 9950X3D 16-Core Processor (32 threads) - 62.33 GiB memory - PulseAudio (44100 Hz, Stereo/mono)

I could also reproduce this on 4.7.beta1 and 4.7.beta2 official builds. I bisected the regression to https://github.com/godotengine/godot/pull/109458.

This has nothing to do with changing the parameters. The editor hangs because the font resource picker is in the tree and it's spammed with update requests (which are expensive due to resource preview stuff). Restarting the project clears the inspector, so the theme is no longer edited, so there is no resource picker to update.

